<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Distance along the equator</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Two ships are on the equator at [[if test="is(ix=1)"]]\(12^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\) and \(47^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=2)"]]\(25^\circ\,\mathrm{W}\) and \(40^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=3)"]]\(150^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\) and \(120^\circ\,\mathrm{W}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=4)"]]\(8^\circ\,\mathrm{W}\) and \(62^\circ\,\mathrm{W}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=5)"]]\(33^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\) and \(108^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=6)"]]\(70^\circ\,\mathrm{W}\) and \(95^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=7)"]]\(165^\circ\,\mathrm{W}\) and \(140^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=8)"]]\(18^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\) and \(77^\circ\,\mathrm{W}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=9)"]]\(100^\circ\,\mathrm{W}\) and \(130^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\)[[/if]][[if test="is(ix=10)"]]\(3^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\) and \(58^\circ\,\mathrm{E}\)[[/if]]. Take \(\pi = \tfrac{22}{7}\) and \(R = 6370\,\text{km}\). Distances in km within 0.1% of the exact value are accepted.</p><p>(a) Find the shorter distance between them along the equator, in nautical miles.</p><p>[[input:ans1]] nm [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p><p>(b) Find this distance in kilometres, to the nearest km.</p><p>[[input:ans2]] km [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) [[if test="same"]]Both ships are on the same side of the Greenwich meridian, so subtract the longitudes.[[else]]The ships are on opposite sides of the Greenwich meridian, so add the longitudes.[[/if]] [[if test="same"]]\(|{@l1@}^\circ - {@l2@}^\circ| = {@ang@}^\circ\).[[else]]\({@l1@}^\circ + {@l2@}^\circ = {@sm@}^\circ\)[[if test="ov180"]], which is more than \(180^\circ\), so the shorter arc goes the other way round, across the \(180^\circ\) meridian: \(360^\circ - {@sm@}^\circ = {@ang@}^\circ\).[[else]].[[/if]][[/if]] The equator is a great circle, so the distance in nm is the angle in minutes: \({@ang@} \times 60 = {@nm@}\) nm.</p><p>(b) \[\frac{ {@ang@} }{360} \times 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 6370 = \frac{ {@ang@} }{360} \times 40\,040 = {@dispdp(float(kmv),1)@} \approx {@floor(kmv+1/2)@}\,\text{km}.\]</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[cosd(x) := cos(x*%pi/180);
sind(x) := sin(x*%pi/180);
atand(x) := 180*atan(x)/%pi;
asind(x) := 180*asin(x)/%pi;
two(v) := if v < 10 then sconcat("0", string(v)) else string(v);
hm(m) := block([q], q: mod(m, 1440), sconcat(two(floor(q/60)), ":", two(mod(q, 60))));
dmx(m) := sconcat(string(floor(m/60)), "^\\circ", two(mod(m, 60)), "'");
/* Design: distance along the equator, including the way round via 180. [lon1, E/W, lon2, E/W]. The long-way node (360 - angle) applies on every entry. Source: adapted from SMP Book 5 (Metric), chapter 3, section 6, Example 1 and Exercise D question 3. */
L: [[12,"E",47,"E"],[25,"W",40,"E"],[150,"E",120,"W"],[8,"W",62,"W"],[33,"E",108,"E"],[70,"W",95,"E"],[165,"W",140,"E"],[18,"E",77,"W"],[100,"W",130,"E"],[3,"E",58,"E"]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
E: L[ix];
l1: E[1]; l2: E[3]; same: is(E[2]=E[4]);
sm: l1+l2;
ov180: is(not same and sm>180);
ang: if same then abs(l1-l2) else (if sm>180 then 360-sm else sm);
nm: 60*ang;
kmv: ang/360*40040;
kmtol: max(1,kmv/1000);
/* diagnostics */
ov: 60*(360-ang);
wr: 60*(if same then sm else abs(l1-l2));
deg: ang;
half: kmv/2;
divnm: nm/1.853;
]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
